Ask people in Slack to review any open GitHub pull request. It posts a card with the pull request's title, your `inline_summary`, and an "I'll review" button in the repository's review channel (`reviewChannel` in `.open-swe/settings.json`). Anyone may sign up, and the pull request merges on its own once every reviewer approves on GitHub, or two hours after the request with at least one approval, as long as checks pass and nothing is unresolved.

Use it when someone asks for a human review, or when a pull request you opened is ready for one. It is refused while the pull request is a draft, has merge conflicts, or is failing a required check. When the pull request is a draft, refuse the review request regardless of who asks. Give them the PR's canonical URL and ask its author to review the changes carefully themselves before clicking **Mark ready** in Slack or **Ready for review** on GitHub. Never undraft it yourself or request a review until the author has clicked the button. For a tiny change that someone can approve straight from the diff, use `expedite_pr_approval` instead.

If a blocker other than draft status prevents the review request, investigate and resolve it within the requested scope when safe, verify and push any fixes, then retry this tool. Do not merely report that the PR is blocked, whether by merge conflicts, failing checks, or another fixable prerequisite; draft status is never yours to change, and only the author's readiness button click may undraft the PR. If you need access, authorization, or a behavioral decision, explain the specific blocker and offer to fix it or ask for the concrete action needed to proceed. Never bypass safeguards or force-push without explicit authorization.

`inline_summary` is required: one or two plain sentences, at most 280 characters, on what the change does and why, as a reviewer wants to know before opening it. Write it only from the pull request itself: {% if not mcp %}from what you wrote when this thread opened it, or otherwise {% endif %}from `gh pr view <url> --json title,body` and `gh pr diff <url>` read in this run; never from the branch name, the title alone, or what you remember of other pull requests. No pull request numbers, URLs, SHAs, file paths or names, and no request to review it.

Pass `channel` (a Slack channel name like `#eng-reviews` or a channel id) to post somewhere other than the repository's review channel; it is required when the repository has none. {% if mcp %}The card is posted in that channel and the person you are working for is sent a Slack DM pointing to it.{% else %}When this conversation is already a thread in that channel, the card is posted in the thread and also sent to the channel; otherwise it is posted in that channel and this thread gets a one-line pointer to it.{% endif +%}

The card is the announcement. Do not follow it with a status update, and never link to the card: Slack unfurls that link into a second copy of it.

{% if mcp %}If nobody signs up within 2 hours (or the workspace’s configured timeout) Open SWE picks a reviewer itself; use `assign_human_reviewer` to name one sooner. Calling this again for a pull request with an open request returns the existing card; when the request came from the person you are working for, the card's summary is replaced with the new `inline_summary`, which is how you correct it.{% else %}If nobody signs up within 2 hours (or the workspace’s configured timeout) you are woken to pick a reviewer with `assign_human_reviewer`. Calling this again for a pull request with an open request returns the existing card; when the request came from this thread or the person you are working for, the card's summary is replaced with the new `inline_summary`, which is how you correct it.{% endif %} To take the request down instead, use `dismiss_human_review_request`.
